WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.333
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:01.333 --> 00:00:02.416
What's up?

00:00:02.416 --> 00:00:03.870
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Dina.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.930
I heard you have made one of
our company's internal web apps

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.960
serverless up on
Google Cloud platform.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.850
Pete in the ops
team said that it's

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.650
much easier to manage that
app now that it's serverless?

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.900
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh, yeah.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.910
And it was pretty
easy to move it.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.180
And because it's serverless
now, it requires less ops work

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.860
to keep it going.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.180
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, good.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.420
So our company's web app
for gathering user feedback

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.340
is starting to get
more traffic now

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.040
that life is
returning to normal.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.810
And the app went down yesterday.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.820
And I'm starting
to feel overwhelmed

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.390
by the ops work required to
keep it running, and restarting

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.770
it, and everything.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.038
Could you help me
make it serverless?

00:00:43.038 --> 00:00:44.580
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
I would love to.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.957
Let's do it.

00:00:45.957 --> 00:00:48.819
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.070
All right.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.000
I remember hearing about
the user feedback web app.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.690
We use it to ask our
customers questions, right?

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.580
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yep, and let's see.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.870
Here it is.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.640
Here's a sample question.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.300
Ask your customers what lollipop
flavor we should release next?

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.550
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh, gosh.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.190
Who's voting for kale?

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:16.690
Ugh.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.392
Anyway, how does the app work?

00:01:22.392 --> 00:01:23.850
MARTIN OMANDER: So
it's a container

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.890
that runs on a virtual machine,
which is backed by a MongoDB

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.180
database instance in the Cloud.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.430
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh, yeah.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.010
I remember moving
that MongoDB instance

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.363
to the Cloud a
couple of years ago.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:38.280
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.390
And back in 2019, the app
got a lot more traffic

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.970
after our new product
hit the stores.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.180
So it crashed, and no
one was around to restart

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.240
the virtual machine.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.090
So it remained down
for a few days.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.625
And we can't have
that happen again.

00:01:52.625 --> 00:01:54.000
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Oh, my gosh.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.020
I remember that.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.560
Ugh.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.495
Also, aren't you paying for
that virtual machine 24/7?

00:02:01.495 --> 00:02:03.370
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
that's the other thing.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.020
Sometimes, we get
a lot of traffic.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.840
And the app needs more
than one virtual machine.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.240
But sometimes, no one is
using it for days at a time.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.990
And we run many virtual
machines for this app--

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:19.030
one for production, one for
staging, a few for development.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:22.270
And it's expensive to pay
for all those idle servers

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.030
or to assign someone to
turn them on and off when

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.320
they aren't needed.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.010
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Oh, I completely agree.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.430
OK, let's start by
determining whether or not

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.120
your app is a good fit
for serverless hosting.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.700
And I have a checklist.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.150
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that sounds great.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.130
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
OK, item number one,

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.850
is your application a web app?

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.200
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, it is.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.658
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Ah, excellent.

00:02:49.658 --> 00:02:54.860
OK, item number two, is your
application containerized?

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.418
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, it sure is.

00:02:56.418 --> 00:02:57.710
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh, great.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:02.240
OK, item number three, is
the container stateless?

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.390
That is, it doesn't store
user sessions and RAM

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.418
between requests or
anything like that?

00:03:08.418 --> 00:03:10.460
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
there are no user sessions,

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.310
or all the user sessions
are stored in the database.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:16.503
All the data is in the database,
nothing in temporary variables

00:03:16.503 --> 00:03:17.420
or anything like that.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:18.628
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Perfect.

00:03:18.628 --> 00:03:21.680
OK, this sounds like a
good fit for Cloud Run

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.420
because it is a stateless
web app in a container.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.910
App Engine is a great
fit for web apps,

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:30.980
but it doesn't use containers.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.680
And honestly, this sounds
a little too complex

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.392
for Cloud Functions.

00:03:35.392 --> 00:03:36.350
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, OK.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.120
Let's deploy it
on Cloud Run then.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.520
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yes,
though one moment, one note.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.630
To be clear--

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:42.680
I just want to be clear--

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.440
the application doesn't have
to be in a stateless container

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.540
right now to move
it to Cloud Run,

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.300
but it would require
more steps if it weren't.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.150
On the other hand, your use
case is very straightforward.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.500
So let's do it.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.120
Let's deploy your existing
container to Cloud Run.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.680
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, let's see.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.160
I'm going to Cloud Run
here in the console.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.250
I click here to
create a new service.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:10.940
I give it a name and region.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:15.530
I pick my existing container
from the Container Registry.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.240
OK, so far, so good.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.250
But Dina, doesn't
the container need

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.280
to be adapted somehow
for Cloud Run?

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.750
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: It does.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:25.370
Great question.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.550
I've moved a few web apps from
virtual machines to Cloud Run.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:32.980
And I've created a
short checklist for it.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.080
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, you
really do like checklists,

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.545
don't you, Dina?

00:04:36.545 --> 00:04:38.170
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Guilty as charged.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:39.190
I love them.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.140
OK, here it is.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.220
Variables &amp; secrets,
connections, security,

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.800
and trigger, let's start
with the first item.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.910
Click Advanced Settings and
then Variables &amp; Secrets.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:57.030
Click Add Variable,
and then you just

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:58.920
add all the
environment variables

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.592
that your container expects.

00:05:01.592 --> 00:05:02.550
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.910
So the virtual machine has
five environmental variables

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.920
that are used by the container.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.295
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Yeah, you're

00:05:09.295 --> 00:05:11.760
just going to copy all
of those to Cloud Run,

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.240
but not the port variable
because Cloud Run does

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.310
that for you.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.190
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
give me a minute.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.450
All right.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:29.800
OK, so what's this
Secret sections here?

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.300
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: If there
are some environment variables

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.030
that are extra sensitive
like passwords,

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:37.970
you can put them in Google
Cloud's Secret Manager.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.930
That way, you get fine-grained
permissions per secret,

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.480
audit logs of who reads
and writes the secrets,

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.760
and versioning of the secrets.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.370
It's pretty useful.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.100
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, that
does sound pretty useful.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:56.270
But I don't want to update
the code in my container,

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.390
and retest the container,
and rebuild the container.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.640
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Oh,
that's OK, you don't have to.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.520
If you've already defined
these as environment variables

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.450
in your container, you can use
the Cloud Run configuration

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:12.050
to expose the secrets from
Secret Manager as environment

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.790
variables.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.100
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that sounds great.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.850
I will check that out later.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.550
For now, let's just
keep it simple.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.830
I will use these
normal environment

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:22.945
variables I have here.

00:06:22.945 --> 00:06:24.320
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Sounds good.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.680
The next item on my
checklist is connections.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.280
Does your container connect
to any virtual machines

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.050
in a Virtual Private
Cloud, or VPC?

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.480
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, it
does connect to that MongoDB

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.440
virtual machine.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.440
And that one only has
an internal IP address.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.893
It's not visible on the
external public internet.

00:06:44.893 --> 00:06:46.060
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Got it.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:48.370
OK, so that MongoDB
virtual machine

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.320
sits in a virtual private cloud.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.860
When your container was also
running on a virtual machine,

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.280
it was in the VPC, and it
could connect to the MongoDB

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.640
by default. For
Cloud Run, we need

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.370
to add that VPC connection.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:07.330
So you're going to
click Connections,

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:10.720
then create a Serverless
VPC Connector,

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.840
then Create connector.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.170
Go ahead and give it a name.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:19.470
Under Subnet, pick
Custom IP Range,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.450
and then enter that
default that they're

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:25.050
showing there, 10.8.0.0.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:28.080
Click Create, and
the new connector

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.160
will be available
in a minute or two.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.920
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
I did all of that.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.298
OK, let's wait for it.

00:07:36.298 --> 00:07:38.840
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: OK, once
the connector has been created,

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.090
go back to the Cloud
Run page, and refresh

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:43.250
that list of VPC connectors.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.010
Now pick the one that
you just created.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:48.740
MARTIN OMANDER: All
right, very good.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:50.100
Done.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:55.280
OK, so that was number two
on your four-item checklist.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.620
What's the next one?

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.198
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Ah,
the next one is security.

00:07:59.198 --> 00:08:00.740
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
well as you know,

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.710
Dina, security is
important to our company.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:07.147
How should I set
up strong security?

00:08:07.147 --> 00:08:08.730
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
You should always

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:11.430
use the principle
of least privilege.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:14.670
This container should only have
the minimum privileges needed

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:17.970
to do its job and no more.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:21.090
You could run this container
as the default compute engine

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:24.150
service account, but that
account has more permissions

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.140
than your container needs.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:29.340
So go to the Security tab,
and click Create New Service

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.420
Account.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:34.020
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, I
see that the system here

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.880
suggested a name for
this new service account.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.010
It looks like it's based on the
name of the Cloud Run service.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:41.610
That's nice.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.310
That will make it easy
to keep track of things.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:46.860
I'm clicking Create here.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:50.093
Oh, should I grant it any
additional permissions here?

00:08:50.093 --> 00:08:51.760
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Does your container

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:55.030
access any other
Google Cloud products?

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:58.048
MARTIN OMANDER: No, it only
accesses that MongoDB instance.

00:08:58.048 --> 00:08:59.590
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
OK, then there's

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:02.300
no need to grant
additional permissions.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:03.130
And that's good.

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.390
This is a good thing.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.490
If a coworker of
ours accidentally

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.380
adds code to the
container that tries

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:14.020
to access any other resource
in this project, it will fail.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:17.020
And that is safer for everybody.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:19.840
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
that does feel good.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.550
OK, so far, so good.

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:23.883
What's the fourth item
on your checklist?

00:09:23.883 --> 00:09:25.300
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
It's trigger.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.890
So we need to configure how
the service is triggered.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:29.410
So go ahead and
click that section,

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.900
and we'll walk through it.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.280
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, done.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:34.780
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
First, you need

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:38.050
to set which networks
can access the service.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:42.630
If it's a public web app, you
want to pick Allow All Traffic.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.030
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, so
I'm leaving that as it is.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.940
What are the two other
radio buttons for, Dina?

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.490
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: The second
radio button, Allow Internal

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:53.070
Traffic and Traffic From
Cloud Load Balancing,

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:54.780
is for apps that
get their traffic

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.440
from another internal
application or via a load

00:09:58.440 --> 00:09:59.550
balancer.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.890
You need to put a load balancer
in front of your service

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.630
to use Cloud Armor
for protection

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:07.800
or Identity-Aware Proxy
for authentication.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:09.925
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it,
and the third radio button?

00:10:09.925 --> 00:10:11.383
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Oh, that one's

00:10:11.383 --> 00:10:14.100
useful if your service is an
internal microservice that

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:17.770
should only be reachable by
other services in your project.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:19.320
If you pick that
option, your service

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:23.290
will not be visible to
the public internet.

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:24.780
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, got it.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:25.280
Let's see.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:28.280
The next section here
says Authentication.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:29.873
What should I pick there?

00:10:29.873 --> 00:10:31.790
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Well, this app is public,

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:35.570
so you should stick to
the first radio button.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.720
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah,
OK, I'll do that.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.692
But what would happen if I
picked the second radio button?

00:10:40.692 --> 00:10:42.900
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Well,
then only service accounts

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:44.900
which have the Cloud
Run Invoker permission

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:46.490
would be able to
trigger your service.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:50.000
And the public wouldn't
be able to use it at all.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.530
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, got it.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:52.708
Is that it?

00:10:52.708 --> 00:10:53.750
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yes.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:56.240
Click the Create button.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:57.980
MARTIN OMANDER: Ooh, all right.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:10:59.012
Clicking it now.

00:10:59.012 --> 00:11:00.470
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
OK, let's wait

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.470
for the deployment to finish.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:04.780
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.730
It looks like the
deployment is done.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:08.000
That was quick.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:08.675
And what now?

00:11:08.675 --> 00:11:11.050
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Cloud
Run has given your new service

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:11.920
a URL.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:14.697
Do you see it there
in the Cloud Console?

00:11:14.697 --> 00:11:16.030
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, there it is.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.145
I'm clicking it.

00:11:17.145 --> 00:11:18.520
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
And there it

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.210
is, your existing container
running serverlessly

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:23.890
in Cloud Run.

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:26.740
As a side note, if you wanted a
different URL for your service,

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:29.980
you could set that up by
clicking Custom Domain.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.520
MARTIN OMANDER: Nice, and
the application is working.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.190
So to recap, what
was your checklist

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:40.318
for moving an existing container
serverlessly to Cloud Run?

00:11:40.318 --> 00:11:41.860
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
OK, first, check

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:44.050
what environment variables
your container is using,

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:46.510
and enter them in the
Cloud Run user interface.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.400
You can also set
them from the command

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:50.410
line with the gcloud command.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.390
For sensitive data,
consider putting them

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:55.810
in Secret Manager for added
security and auditing.

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:57.670
And you can still
access these values

00:11:57.670 --> 00:11:59.060
as environment variables.

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:02.820
So you won't have to edit the
code inside your container.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:04.150
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.760
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Then if
your container accesses servers

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:10.930
within a virtual private cloud,
like a database, for instance,

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:14.450
set up a serverless VPC
connector to the VPC

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:17.410
where your server lives.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:18.430
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:20.263
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
After that is security.

00:12:20.263 --> 00:12:21.700
Create a new
service account that

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:25.030
has the minimum
privileges needed to let

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:27.490
the container do its job.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:30.835
Configure Cloud Run to use
that new service account.

00:12:30.835 --> 00:12:32.950
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, yeah,
I remember doing that.

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:34.450
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
And finally, we

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:36.730
need to configure how
the service is triggered.

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:40.510
If it's a public web app,
pick Allow All Traffic.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:43.810
If your app gets traffic from
the Google Cloud load balancer,

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:45.530
you'd pick that option.

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:49.180
And if your service is an
internal-only service that

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:51.820
should not be reachable
from the public internet,

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:53.847
pick the option for that.

00:12:53.847 --> 00:12:54.930
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.300
This checklist will
make it a lot easier

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.550
to move other
containers to Cloud Run

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:03.440
so I don't have to maintain
those pesky servers.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.050
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: And you
get autoscaling with Cloud Run.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:10.430
So if the thousands of customers
want to vote on our next candy

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:13.070
flavor, the container
will automatically

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:15.620
be deployed to more instances.

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:21.260
And bonus, you're not paying
for it when it's not in use.

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:24.950
MARTIN OMANDER: That sounds like
the best kind of flavor to me.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:26.760
Thank you, everyone,
for watching.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:30.140
If you have questions for me
or Dina about moving containers

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:33.000
to Cloud Run, please let
us know in the comments.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.760
Also, if you have suggestions
for future videos,

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:37.730
please enter them below.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:38.330
Bye.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:41.680
